---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import { projects } from "../../data/projects";
import { activities, kit, courseUrl } from "../../data/course";

const project = projects.find((p) => p.id === "cryptography")!;

/** Verified against the course's current curriculum file: 16 sections, 43 lectures. */
const sections = [
  "Why cryptography matters in software",
  "Just enough math",
  "Classical ciphers and the pattern problem",
  "Symmetric encryption and AES",
  "Hashing and file integrity",
  "Password security",
  "Public key cryptography",
  "Digital signatures",
  "Certificates, PKI, TLS, and HTTPS",
  "JWTs and modern authentication tokens",
  "Randomness, keys, and common mistakes",
  "Real-world cryptography failures",
  "The crypto testing checklist",
  "Practical test cases",
  "Capstone: secure document exchange",
  "The future of cryptography",
];
---

<Base
  title="Practical Cryptography, Jamal Mammadzada"
  description="A case study on a 43-lecture cryptography course that ends with a broken application students have to audit and decide whether to ship."
  progress={true}
>
  <article>
    <header class="wrap case-head">
      <p class="mono">
        <a href="/work/">Work</a> / Case study {project.index}
      </p>
      <h1 class="h-xl case-title">Practical Cryptography</h1>
      <p class="lede narrow case-lede">
        A course for developers and QA engineers, built around a problem most
        cryptography teaching avoids: deciding whether software that uses
        cryptography is safe to ship.
      </p>

      <dl class="meta-grid case-meta">
        {
          project.meta.map((m) => (
            <div>
              <dt>{m.label}</dt>
              <dd>{m.value}</dd>
            </div>
          ))
        }
        <div>
          <dt>Released</dt>
          <dd>2026</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Course</dt>
          <dd>
            {
              courseUrl ? (
                <a href={courseUrl} rel="noopener">
                  Practical Cryptography for Developers and QA Engineers
                </a>
              ) : (
                <span class="dim">Listing link to be added</span>
              )
            }
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>

      <p class="note case-note">
        Instructor materials, hidden assessment tests, and the capstone source
        aren't published here. The capstone is intentionally vulnerable training
        software and is meant to run only on a student's own machine.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="wrap section narrow" aria-labelledby="gap">
      <h2 class="h-lg sec-title" id="gap">The gap this is aimed at</h2>

      <p>
        A developer can pass a cryptography quiz and still ship something broken.
        They know AES is a block cipher and that you shouldn't reuse an IV, and
        then they read code that reuses one and don't notice, because recognizing
        the mistake in unfamiliar software is a different skill from reciting the
        rule.
      </p>

      <p>
        Almost nothing about real cryptographic failure is about the algorithms.
        AES doesn't break. What breaks is everything around it: a mode chosen for
        the wrong reason, a nonce derived predictably, a signature verified in a
        code path nobody takes, a token whose algorithm field is trusted. Those are integration and review failures, and you don't catch
        them by knowing definitions.
      </p>

      <p>
        So the course is built backwards from what a working engineer has to
        do: look at a system, work out where cryptography appears
        in it, test whether it holds, and say whether it ships.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="wrap section" aria-labelledby="shape">
      <h2 class="h-lg sec-title narrow" id="shape">Shape of the curriculum</h2>

      <p class="narrow">
        43 lectures across 16 sections. The first half builds the mechanics; the
        second half is progressively less about how primitives work and more
        about how they get misused.
      </p>

      <ol class="sections" role="list">
        {
          sections.map((s, i) => (
            <li class="reveal">
              <span class="sec-n mono">{String(i + 1).padStart(2, "0")}</span>
              <span>{s}</span>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ol>

      <p class="narrow sections-after">
        Heartbleed, DigiNotar, and the rest get whole sections because a student who has seen how a real system failed
        reads unfamiliar code differently than one who has only seen it work.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="wrap section" aria-labelledby="rendered-lessons">
      <h2 class="h-lg sec-title narrow" id="rendered-lessons">Ideas built to move</h2>

      <p class="narrow lesson-intro">
        The lecture pipeline renders concepts as timed visual explanations, not
        screenshot-heavy slides. These frames come directly from the course's
        render checks; each one has to carry the argument even with the narration muted.
      </p>

      <div class="lesson-frames">
        <figure class="reveal">
          <img
            src="/img/cryptography/birthday-effect.png"
            alt="Course frame explaining that finding any matching pair takes roughly the square root of the work of hitting one fixed target."
            width="1280"
            height="720"
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <figcaption><span class="mono">Collision resistance</span>The birthday effect turns an abstract exponent into a comparison students can see.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure class="reveal">
          <img
            src="/img/cryptography/md5-threat-model.png"
            alt="Course frame comparing accidental corruption with attacker-chosen input when deciding whether MD5 is acceptable."
            width="1280"
            height="720"
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <figcaption><span class="mono">Threat modeling</span>The useful question is not only which algorithm, but who controls the input.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure class="reveal">
          <img
            src="/img/cryptography/modern-primitives.png"
            alt="Course frame mapping file integrity, signatures, passwords, and keyed hashes to modern cryptographic choices."
            width="1280"
            height="720"
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <figcaption><span class="mono">Replacement choices</span>Different jobs need different primitives; “use SHA-256” is not a complete rule.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="wrap section narrow" aria-labelledby="capstone">
      <h2 class="h-lg sec-title" id="capstone">The capstone</h2>

      <p>
        Students are handed a release candidate: a production-shaped B2B document
        portal with an API, a browser client, a TLS gateway, and cryptography
        threaded through it in the places you'd expect. It comes with a smoke
        suite that passes.
      </p>

      <p>
        That last detail is the whole design. The supplied tests prove the
        ordinary workflows run. They prove nothing about whether the release is
        secure, and the course is explicit that students shouldn't confuse the
        two. A green test suite on insecure software is exactly the situation
        this is training for.
      </p>

      <p>The assignment is the real job, in order:</p>

      <ul class="task-list" role="list">
        <li>Find where cryptography appears in a system nobody explained to you.</li>
        <li>Build a testing framework specific to what this system actually does.</li>
        <li>Write and run executable tests. Running code, not a written review.</li>
        <li>Preserve evidence for the findings.</li>
        <li>Report what's wrong, with enough support that someone else can act on it.</li>
        <li>Make a defensible release decision, and defend it.</li>
      </ul>

      <details class="tech capstone-note">
        <summary>Why the defects are planted on purpose</summary>
        <p>
          Because I know every defect in the application, there's a ground
          truth for what a thorough audit should surface. A student's work gets
          measured against what's there, not against how confident their report
          sounds.
        </p>
        <p>
          It also means I got to pick the failure modes. They come from the
          categories that cause real incidents, not the ones that are convenient
          to hide.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="tech">
        <summary>Grading a security audit without a right answer key</summary>
        <p>
          Assessment runs against hidden tests rather than a rubric a student
          can pattern-match. Alongside that, mutation-based evaluation checks
          something a checklist can't: whether a student's test suite detects
          the problems it claims to cover.
        </p>
        <p>
          A test that passes on broken code is worse than no test, because it
          produces confidence. Mutating the system and checking that the
          student's tests fail is how you find that out.
        </p>
      </details>
    </section>

    <section class="wrap section" aria-labelledby="range">
      <h2 class="h-lg sec-title narrow" id="range">The activity kit</h2>

      <p class="narrow">
        Lectures alone don't teach you to spot a broken implementation, so the
        course ships a local range: {kit.activities} graded exercises plus an
        orientation, roughly {Math.round(kit.minutes / 60)} hours of work, placed
        at the points in the course where a new skill becomes testable.
      </p>

      <p class="narrow">
        It borrows the rhythm of a cyber competition. Read a scenario, inspect a
        real artifact on your own machine, make a decision you have to defend,
        repair something when the exercise asks for it, and watch the score
        report update. No virtual machine, no cloud account, and nothing that
        needs production credentials or a system you don't own.
      </p>

      <div class="kit-grid">
        {
          activities.map((a) => (
            <div class="kit-item reveal">
              <div class="kit-top">
                <span class="mono kit-n">{a.n}</span>
                <span class="mono kit-min">{a.minutes}m</span>
              </div>
              <h3 class="kit-title">{a.title}</h3>
              <p class="kit-format">{a.format}</p>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>

      <div class="narrow">
        <p class="kit-note">
          Sixteen exercises, sixteen different formats. A key-ceremony tabletop
          and a mutation-test design exercise assess different things, and
          running everything as the same kind of quiz would flatten that.
        </p>

        <details class="tech">
          <summary>Why the range runs on your own machine</summary>
          <p>
            Every exercise runs from an extracted folder with a private Python
            environment, on Python {kit.python}. Setup is one script, and it
            prints READY or tells you what's wrong.
          </p>
          <p>
            The reason is access. A course that needs a cloud account or a VM
            image excludes people, breaks when a provider changes, and puts a
            billing question between a student and the material. A folder and a
            Python install keeps working.
          </p>
        </details>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="wrap section narrow" aria-labelledby="decision">
      <h2 class="h-lg sec-title" id="decision">The release decision</h2>

      <p>
        The last deliverable is a judgment: does this ship, and why?
      </p>

      <p>
        That's the part I most wanted in the course, because it's the part
        engineers get wrong and no exam tests. Not every defect blocks a release,
        and "I found something" is not a decision. The skill is working out
        which findings block, which are acceptable with a known risk, and how to
        say so to someone who wants to ship on Friday. That's what separates
        someone who can run a scanner from someone you'd trust to sign off.
      </p>

      <p>
        It's also the same idea underneath{" "}
        <a href="/work/frcmob/">FRCMOB's</a> review loop and the simulate-first
        design of the{" "}
        <a href="/work/hardening/">hardening framework</a>: an automated result
        is evidence for a decision, not the decision.
      </p>
    </section>

    {
      courseUrl && (
        <section class="wrap section" aria-labelledby="take">
          <div class="course-cta">
            <div>
              <p class="mono">Available on Udemy</p>
              <h2 class="h-md" id="take">
                Practical Cryptography for Developers and QA Engineers
              </h2>
              <p class="course-sub">
                43 lectures, 16 sections, and the full activity kit.
              </p>
            </div>
            <a href={courseUrl} class="btn" rel="noopener">View the course</a>
          </div>
        </section>
      )
    }

    <nav class="wrap section case-next" aria-label="More work">
      <a href="/work/frc5417/" class="next-link">
        <span class="mono">Next case study</span>
        <span class="h-md">FRC Team 5417 →</span>
      </a>
    </nav>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .case-head {
    padding-top: clamp(2.5rem, 6vw, 4.5rem);
  }

  .case-head > .mono {
    margin-bottom: 1.4rem;
  }

  .case-title {
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
  }

  .case-lede {
    margin-bottom: 2.5rem;
  }

  .case-meta {
    max-width: 34rem;
    margin-bottom: 1.75rem;
  }

  .case-note {
    max-width: 60ch;
  }

  .sec-title {
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
  }

  .sections {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 2rem 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(260px, 1fr));
    gap: 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  .sections li {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 2.4rem 1fr;
    gap: 0.6rem;
    align-items: baseline;
    padding: 0.62rem 1rem 0.62rem 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
    font-size: var(--fs-ui);
    color: var(--ink-2);
  }

  .sec-n {
    font-size: var(--fs-label);
    color: var(--accent);
  }

  .sections-after {
    margin-top: 2rem;
  }

  .task-list {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 1.25rem 0 2rem;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.55rem;
    counter-reset: task;
  }

  .task-list li {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1.6rem 1fr;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    color: var(--ink-2);
    font-size: var(--fs-ui);
    counter-increment: task;
  }

  .task-list li::before {
    content: counter(task, decimal-leading-zero);
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: var(--fs-label);
    color: var(--accent);
    padding-top: 0.35em;
  }

  .capstone-note {
    margin-top: 1rem;
  }

  /* 16 items. auto-fill leaves a ragged last row at some widths, so the
     column count is pinned to divisors of 16 and the grid stays complete. */
  .kit-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr);
    gap: 1px;
    background: var(--rule);
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    margin: 2.25rem 0;
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  .lesson-intro {
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
  }

  .lesson-frames {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(12, 1fr);
    gap: clamp(1rem, 2.5vw, 2rem);
  }

  .lesson-frames figure {
    grid-column: span 7;
    margin: 0;
  }

  .lesson-frames figure:nth-child(even) {
    grid-column: 6 / span 7;
  }

  .lesson-frames img {
    width: 100%;
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    background: #0b2230;
  }

  .lesson-frames figcaption {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.45rem;
    margin-top: 0.7rem;
    color: var(--ink-2);
    font-size: var(--fs-sm);
    line-height: 1.5;
  }

  .lesson-frames figcaption .mono {
    color: var(--accent);
    font-size: var(--fs-label);
  }

  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .kit-grid {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
    }

    .lesson-frames figure,
    .lesson-frames figure:nth-child(even) {
      grid-column: 1 / -1;
    }
  }

  @media (max-width: 460px) {
    .kit-grid {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }

  .kit-item {
    background: var(--paper-2);
    padding: 0.9rem 1rem 1rem;
  }

  .kit-top {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 0.6rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
  }

  .kit-n {
    color: var(--accent);
    font-size: var(--fs-label);
  }

  .kit-min {
    font-size: var(--fs-label);
  }

  .kit-title {
    margin: 0 0 0.3rem;
    font-size: var(--fs-ui);
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: -0.012em;
  }

  .kit-format {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: var(--fs-sm);
    color: var(--ink-3);
    line-height: 1.4;
  }

  .kit-note {
    margin-bottom: 1.75rem;
  }

  .course-cta {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 1.5rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    background: var(--paper-2);
    padding: 1.6rem 1.5rem;
  }

  .course-cta .mono {
    margin: 0 0 0.5rem;
    color: var(--accent);
  }

  .course-cta h2 {
    max-width: 28ch;
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
  }

  .course-sub {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: var(--fs-ui);
    color: var(--ink-3);
  }

  .case-next {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  .next-link {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    text-decoration: none;
    color: inherit;
    padding: 1.5rem 0;
  }

  .next-link .h-md {
    transition: color 0.16s ease;
  }

  .next-link:hover .h-md {
    color: var(--accent);
  }
</style>
